{"id": "q044", "split": "test", "mode": "rag", "version": "7cb1b414db4c", "question": "I wrote a kubectl plugin named after a built-in kubectl command, but it never runs. Why?", "gold": "Plugins can't override existing kubectl commands; the built-in command always wins", "answerable": true, "source_file": "tasks/extend-kubectl/kubectl-plugins.md", "retrieved": [{"chunk_id": "tasks/extend-kubectl/kubectl-plugins.md#6", "doc_path": "tasks/extend-kubectl/kubectl-plugins.md", "score": 0.9090167880058289, "n_tokens": 510}, {"chunk_id": "tasks/extend-kubectl/kubectl-plugins.md#1", "doc_path": "tasks/extend-kubectl/kubectl-plugins.md", "score": 0.9062204360961914, "n_tokens": 510}, {"chunk_id": "tasks/extend-kubectl/kubectl-plugins.md#8", "doc_path": "tasks/extend-kubectl/kubectl-plugins.md", "score": 0.8136199712753296, "n_tokens": 510}], "page_in_top3": true, "evidence_in_top3": true, "answer": "Because it conflicts with a built‑in command. Kubectl will always prefer its own command over a plugin with the same name, so the plugin is ignored.", "answer_usage": {"text": "Because it conflicts with a built‑in command. Kubectl will always prefer its own command over a plugin with the same name, so the plugin is ignored.", "finish_reason": "stop", "prompt_tokens": 1469, "completion_tokens": 66, "total_tokens": 1535, "calls": 1, "errors": []}, "label": "CORRECT", "judge": {"label": "CORRECT", "raw": "CORRECT", "total_tokens": 314, "calls": 1, "errors": []}}
